    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d526">

                <pb facs="0435" n="423" sameAs="frus:frus1942v04/pg423"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.20/585: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Liberia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Hibbard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Monrovia</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-06-22T16:00:00-00:44">June 22, 1942—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 5:13 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>202.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>There remain in Monrovia the Consul as well as Nolke and Brandt who are his
                    non-career assistants and are living with him. I urged the Secretary of State
                    again this morning to request their immediate departure but he is reluctant and
                    I fear will not take action until this Government has received assurance of
                    further protection as outlined in my telegram 198 of June 18, 5 p.m.<anchor
                        n="67" xml:id="d526-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hibbard</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
